  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front will move across the area during \nthe afternoon and shift off the coast early this evening \nresulting in a period of below normal temperatures through the \nfirst part of next week. Frost/freeze potential exists each \nnight this weekend into early next wee. \n\nThe well amplified mid/upper trough in place across the eastern\nU.S. this afternoon has displaced the deeper moisture axis far \noff the Carolinas coast. Water vapor imagery shows dry air in \nplace along the coastal plains but with support aloft and the \nfront passing through may be enough to squeeze out widely \nscattered showers later this afternoon and early evening. While \nthe chances are low, the best chances are across northern \nportions of the forecast area. \n\nThe aforementioned mid/upper trough will linger across the \neastern U.S. through the weekend allowing for below normal \ntemperatures. There is the potential for some frost during the \nearly Saturday morning hours but a lot will depend on wind \nconditions. A better potential exist for frost and freezing \ntemperatures late Saturday night into early Sunday morning, and \nlate Sunday night into early Monday morning. Inland areas have \nthe best potential for freezing temperatures while frost is \npossible even closer to the coast. The risk for frost may extend\neach early morning into the middle of the upcoming week. \nGradually ridging aloft will get temperatures back to near \nnormal for the latter half of the upcoming week.",
